[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. The reports merged into it
do not say that. The desk merged them by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and a subject like "Date is wrong" fits more than one bug. Read by what each report shows, #4471
holds more than one problem. Each problem now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 fixes the shared parser only. The other problems stay open after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

How a report is placed, in this order (`src/lib/kb/problems.ts`):

1. **`one-day`**: the desk read it as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). Its dates are not flipped:
   the booking lands one day early, or before it was made, or on a different day per time zone.
2. **`hint`**: it came in through the booking form of a tenant on `booking-v3`. That form prints
   `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field, so people type the month first, and the form reads it the
   tenant's way, day first. The parser fix does not touch this.
3. **`parser`**: every other report from a day-first tenant. The shared parser reads the date
   month first, so day and month swap.

The tenant decides the problem, not intake's tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and
a reporter at a day-first tenant often has an `en-US` or `en-GB` browser.

## The one-day reports

These show no swapped day and month. They look like a time zone bug, not a date format bug.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

## What the desk should do

- Keep #4471's fix for `parser`. Do not close the other tickets when it ships.
- Fix the hint on `booking-v3` so it matches the tenant's date order, or make the form read what
  the hint asks for.
- Look at time zones for `one-day`: invites built for one zone and sent to another.
- Before you merge a report into a big ticket, read its body. A shared subject line is not a
  shared cause.

